## Personal Experience

Personally I was 8 years old and in second grade (May 1997); I had not long
before learned to read and write. My father brought home an old computer with
Windows 3.1 that had been written off at his work, and it served me to practice
the things from the magazine, although I mainly used it to play DOS games
and learn to type on the keyboard; with that I also learned the basic terminal
commands like creating folders, copying and deleting files, and those DOS
things.

My father was more of an Excel and Word person, so I had to learn all those
things by watching and experimenting on my own.

Even though we didn't have internet at home until 2004, I could go to his
work and download things and more tutorials.

After that he also brought me a photocopied Visual Basic 6 book, where I also
learned to build desktop programs, though super basic ones.

I will finish this small post with the same words from the HTML course of the
Club Nintendo magazine:

> Hopefully these 3 HTML courses will serve you and awaken your interest in
> learning more. If so, there are very good programming books for other
> languages to use on the Internet.
